From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Wed Jul  1 18:02:56 2015
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Wed, 01 Jul 2015 18:02:56 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] problems with implementing custom functions
Message-ID: <C80dnhQqbzU.vOPPZR1xbq4J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <C80dnhQqbzU.7iABY1GX5YkJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <C80dnhQqbzU.7iABY1GX5YkJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/C80dnhQqbzU/m/vOPPZR1xbq4J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Right.  To follow up on what Daniel said, you want to replace
this:

>   increment_log_prob(log(lambda3) - y[N]*lambda3);

with

  increment_log_prob(N * log(lambda3) - sum(y * lambda));

with y declared as

  vector<lower=0>[N] y;

Declaring as vector lets you do vector arithmetic, like multiplying
by a scalar.  Putting the bounds in does error checking on the data
when it's read, which is not required, but is helpful for catching errors.

It's critical that you have the constraints in the parameters
as Daniel stated --- Stan will not sample efficiently (including
perhaps not at all) when you have parameters that have values that
match their declared constraint but have 0 density in the model.

- Bob

