Daniel and Bob,

thanks a lot for your helpful responses!
My results below.

First, I had declared real<lower=0> for lambdas, and stan() function returned a lot of warnings again:

> Informational Message: The current Metropolis proposal is about to be rejected because of the following issue:

> stan::prob::exponential_log(N4stan5agrad3varE): Inverse scale parameter is inf:0, but must be finite!

>
> If this warning occurs sporadically, such as for highly constrained variable types like covariance matrices, then the sampler is fine,
> but if this warning occurs often then your model may be either severely ill-conditioned or misspecified.

Second, I tried both solutions (with loop and vector arithmetics), and all works well. I am delighted!
Look:

y <- rexp(10000, 1.648721)

model_simple <- "
          data {
            int<lower=1> N;
            vector<lower=0>[N] y;
          }
          parameters {
            real<lower=0> lambda1;
            real<lower=0> lambda2;
            real<lower=0> lambda3;
            real<lower=0> lambda4;
          }
          model {
            // solutions from manual

            y ~ exponential(lambda1);
            increment_log_prob(exponential_log(y, lambda2));

            // Daniel's solution

            for (n in 1:N)
                  increment_log_prob(log(lambda3) - y[n]*lambda3);

            // Bob's solution
            increment_log_prob(N * log(lambda4) - sum(y * lambda4));
          }"

mcmc_samples <- stan(model_code = model_simple,
                     data = list(N = length(y), y = y),
                     chains=3, iter=30000, warmup=20000,

                     verbose = F, refresh = -1)

stan() output:

> > mcmc_samples
> Inference for Stan model: model_simple.

> 3 chains, each with iter=30000; warmup=20000; thin=1;
> post-warmup draws per chain=10000, total post-warmup draws=30000.

>
>
>              mean se_mean   sd      2.5%       25%       50%       75%     97.5% n_eff Rhat

> lambda1      1.66    0.00 0.02      1.63      1.65      1.66      1.67      1.69 23921    1
> lambda2      1.66    0.00 0.02      1.63      1.65      1.66      1.67      1.69 23947    1
> lambda3      1.66    0.00 0.02      1.63      1.65      1.66      1.67      1.69 24330    1
> lambda4      1.66    0.00 0.02      1.63      1.65      1.66      1.67      1.69 23789    1
> lp__    -19715.72    0.02 1.45 -19719.39 -19716.45 -19715.39 -19714.65 -19713.90  9111    1

Unfortunately I can't understand the logic of vector arithmetics. Well, I can understand it in simple cases like an exponential function (pdf, of course).
But it is difficult to me to write this (lognormal pdf):

for (n in 1:N)

    increment_log_prob(-log(y[n] * sigma * ml) - pow((log(y[n]) - mu), 2)/(2 * pow(sigma, 2)));

 in vectorized form. Could you help me with it? Where I should use "sum" and multiplication by N, and why?

Thank you so much for the help and advice,
Philipp

On Wednesday, 1 July 2015 21:02:56 UTC+3, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> Right.  To follow up on what Daniel said, you want to replace
> this:
>
> >   increment_log_prob(log(lambda3) - y[N]*lambda3);
>
>
> with
>
>   increment_log_prob(N * log(lambda3) - sum(y * lambda));
>
> with y declared as
>
>   vector<lower=0>[N] y;
>
> Declaring as vector lets you do vector arithmetic, like multiplying
> by a scalar.  Putting the bounds in does error checking on the data
> when it's read, which is not required, but is helpful for catching errors.
>
> It's critical that you have the constraints in the parameters
> as Daniel stated --- Stan will not sample efficiently (including
> perhaps not at all) when you have parameters that have values that
> match their declared constraint but have 0 density in the model.
