Message-ID: <JFShFi5GONo.7maVSgUvE8EJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/JFShFi5GONo/m/7maVSgUvE8EJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

I have a relatively simple hierarchical model:

data{
int<lower=0> N;
int<lower=0> J;
row_vector[2] X[N];
vector[N] y;
int site[N];
matrix[J, 6] U;
matrix[2, 2] R;
}
parameters{
vector[2] B[J];
real<lower=0> sd_y;
cov_matrix[2] tau_b;
matrix[6, 2] Gamma;
}
transformed parameters{
vector[N] yhat;

for(n in 1:N){
yhat[n] <- X[n]*B[site[n]];
}

}
model{
row_vector[2] Bhat[J];
matrix[2,2] L;
for(j in 1:J){
Bhat[j] <- (U[j]*Gamma);
}
L <- cholesky_decompose(tau_b);

y ~ normal(yhat, sd_y) ;
B ~ multi_normal_cholesky(Bhat, L);

// Priors
to_vector(Gamma) ~ normal(0, 1);
sd_y ~ uniform(0, 10);
tau_b ~ wishart(3, R);
}

This runs fairly slowly. Whats more, it always seems like one or two chains gets stuck and take an extremely long to complete sampling. I've tried the Cholesky factorization approach outlined in the manual, but that didn't result in a substantial improvement and still has the same problems with chains getting stuck.

Does anyone have any advice for speeding up the model in general and preventing the chains from getting stuck?

Thanks!
Nate

From u7dd668eb41c5bfd9 at groups.google.com  Tue Jul 14 22:57:25 2015
