Aki, Jonah, and I have released the much-discussed paper on LOO and WAIC in Stan:
http://www.stat.columbia.edu/~gelman/research/unpublished/loo_stan.pdf
We (that is, Aki) now recommend LOO rather than WAIC, especially now that we have an R function to quickly compute LOO using Pareto smoothed importance sampling.

Also 2 new tutorial articles on Stan will be appearing:
in JEBS:  http://www.stat.columbia.edu/~gelman/research/published/stan_jebs_2.pdf
in JSS:  http://www.stat.columbia.edu/~gelman/research/published/stan-paper-revision-feb2015.pdf
The 2 articles have very similar titles but surprisingly little overlap.

I guess I should blog this…

See you
A

From ue1c430550f86b1f6 at groups.google.com  Thu Jul 16 05:19:10 2015
From: ue1c430550f86b1f6 at groups.google.com (andy...@cubist-asia.com)
Date: Thu, 16 Jul 2015 05:19:10 +0000
Subject: Effificent implementation of fitting different priors to the same data set
Message-ID: <QwkENIUJ928.KxZBMn7KtS0J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/QwkENIUJ928/m/KxZBMn7KtS0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear all,

Let's say I want to fit a simple linear model with user supplied prior

data {
  int N;       // some very large value
  vector[N] y;
  vector[N] x;

  real prior_b;
  real prior_b_sd;
}

parameters {
  real b;
  real<lower=0> sigma;
}

model {
  y ~ normal (b * x, sigma);

  b ~ normal (prior_b, prior_b_sd);
}

Question: Now instead of a single set of prior spec, i want to be able to able to compute a set of b's given different priors. The use case can to understand how different (informative) priors can affect b given the same data set.

The data block can look like this:
data{
...
  int<lower=1> N_p
  real prior_b[N_p];
  real prior_b_sd[N_p];
}

But, what's the best way to code up the model block?

A naive approach can be:
parameters {
  real b[N_p];
  real<lower=0> sigma[N_p];
}

model {
  for (i in 1:N_p) {
    y ~ normal (x * b[i], sigma[i]);
  }

  // priors
  b ~ normal (prior_b, prior_b_l);
  a ~ normal (prior_a, prior_a_l);
}

But, this would involve "sampling" N_p times for exactly the same data set (y, x) for the same intermediate results as the difference comes from the different priors.

Is there any way I can compute the log_prob of b using the data set (y, x) once and then apply the prior spec over this value to form each b[i]?

Many thanks,

Andy

From u23a345c7bcfdb75b at groups.google.com  Thu Jul 16 05:46:29 2015
