And a different kind of suggestion to maybe improve performance:

You might be doing this already, but you should make sure your Xs are on a reasonable scale. Definitely center them and it's probably a good idea to divide by 1 (or Andrew says 2 I think) sds. That's if they're continuous. If they're just binary variables then you're fine.

If the Xs are scaled the parameters b1 and b2 are unlikely to be large, so using a smaller scale than 10 for the normal priors would be good.

On Saturday, July 18, 2015, Jonah Sol Gabry <jga...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Yes what you wrote should work, but here are some suggestions:
>
> -instead of binomial_logit(1,p) use bernoulli_logit(p)
> -really the naming would be more clear if you swap the names of p and Yhat.logit
> - maybe use 1/(1+exp(-x)) instead of exp(x)/(1+exp(x)) so you don't do unnecessary exping.
> -extract(fit, pars='p')$p will avoid extracting everything else if you just want p. Same result but should be faster.
>
> Jonah
