No need to do 1/(1+exp(-x)) or exp(x)/(1+exp(x)).  Stan has an inverse logit function.

> On Jul 18, 2015, at 3:25 PM, Jonah Sol Gabry <jga...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Yes what you wrote should work, but here are some suggestions:
>
> -instead of binomial_logit(1,p) use bernoulli_logit(p)
> -really the naming would be more clear if you swap the names of p and Yhat.logit
> - maybe use 1/(1+exp(-x)) instead of exp(x)/(1+exp(x)) so you don't do unnecessary exping.
> -extract(fit, pars='p')$p will avoid extracting everything else if you just want p. Same result but should be faster.
>
> Jonah
>
> On Saturday, July 18, 2015, <judy.b...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Thanks a lot Jonah for your clarification, and Bob for the tip.
> >
> > So, let me see if I get a full logistic model right. Suppose my model is of the form:
> >
> > model{
> >    vector[N] p;
> >    Intercept ~ normal( 0 , 10 );
> >    b1 ~ normal( 0 , 10 );
> >    b2 ~ normal( 0 , 10 );
> >
> >    p <- Intercept + X1 * b1 + X2 * b2;
> >
> >    Y ~ binomial_logit( 1 , p );
> > }
> >
> >
> > generated quantities{
> >     vector[N] p;
> >     real dev;
> >     dev <- 0;
> >     p <- Intercept + X1 * b1 + X2 * b2;
> >     dev <- dev + (-2)*binomial_logit_log( Y, 1 , p );
> > }
> >
> >
> > Then, supposing my fitted model is stored in an object called "fit", in order to calculate WAIC I have to do:
> >
> > Yhat.logit <- exp(extract(fit)$p)/(1+exp(extract(fit)$p))
> > log_lik <- t(sapply(1:nrow(Yhat.logit), function(s) {
> >     dbinom(y, size = 1, prob = Yhat.logit[s, ], log = TRUE)
> >   }))
> > loo(log_lik)
> >
> > Does it look correct?
> >
> > Thanksfor your help again.
> >
> > Judy
> >
> >
> > Em sábado, 18 de julho de 2015 13:26:01 UTC-3, Bob Carpenter escreveu:
> > > If you can declare Yhat, X1, and X as vectors, you can use
> > >
> > >   Yhat <- Intercept + X1 * b + X2 * b;
> > >
> > > It's not going to be any more efficient in generated quantities,
> > > but it's simpler and less error-prone to write.
> > >
> > > - Bob
> > >
> > > > On Jul 17, 2015, at 10:34 PM, judy.b...@gmail.com wrote:
> > > >
> > > > Dear Jonah, thanks for your reply, it's quite on the spot!
> > > >
> > > > Since I am not very experienced in R and Stan (more a direct user of BUGS), let me just clarify some things. First, when you say I could use a custom log-likelihood function in R, I guess it becomes even easier in case I have already saved the Y-hat of the model like in:
> > > >
> > > >  for ( n in 1:N ) {
> > > >      Yhat[n] <- Intercept +X1[n]*b1 + X2[n]*b2;
> > > >    }
> > > >
> > > > Yhat is, therefore, a S by N matrix. I guess I could apply a log-likelihood function to such matrix to get the log_lik and then calculate WAIC following the recently published papers.
> > > >
> > > > I am not sure, however, how to do that in R. I've been reading the help page for the dnorm and dbinomial functions, in order to lear more on how to do it for gaussian and binomial models. However, I am still in a loss. I didn't understand what should go in each parameter of the functions in my case, considering that Yhat is a matrix and considering that is of different dimensions when compared to the output variable, let's call it Y.
> > > >
> > > > For instance, in the gaussian case: should I try something like dnorm( Y , mean=Yhat, sd=extract(fittedmodel)$sigma , log=TRUE )? And for binomial models, would it be like dbinomial(Y,1, Yhat)?
> > > >
> > > > Thanks in advance for your thoughts.
> > > >
> > > > Judy B.
> > > >
> > > >
> > > > Em sábado, 18 de julho de 2015 02:00:55 UTC-3, Jonah escreveu:
> > > > Unfortunately, what you really need is the pointwise log-likelihood. You need to save the log-likelihood evaluated at every data point (for each posterior simulation) instead of adding them up. For Stan this means defining log_lik to be a vector of length N (number of data points). When you extract the draws you'll have an S by N matrix, where S is the number of saved posterior draws.
> > > >
> > > > The good news is that if you can write the log-likelihood function in R then you can evaluate it yourself using the posterior draws of the parameters from your model. Then you could use the loo R package.
> > > >
> > > > Jonah
> > > >
> > > >
