Subject: How to go about using unbalanced panel data with stan?
Message-ID: <gYZc_Yqx1P0.f5Lsh2VBiDsJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/gYZc_Yqx1P0/m/f5Lsh2VBiDsJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I have successfuly run a stan model using unbalanced panel data (organied by country-year) with varying intercepts for each country. However, I would also like to try and include time fixed-effects (or model it hiearchically).

I can do one or the other by sorting my data frame in R, using dplyr and the summarise/group_by function to count the number of rows for each country, taking the cumulative sum of that, and then passing that vector to my stan model by looping over the vector of cumulative sums. This works great.

However I am at a loss as to how to do this for both time and country effects simulatenously, largely because I can only sort my data by either country or year. Any suggestions?

Thanks,
-Rick

For reference, here's the relevant part of how I am doing the country effects:

#first get the number of rows for each country
countrycounts<-summarise(group_by(my_df,country),n=n())

#before I pass it to stan I have to format and subtract off the last entry
numbers<-as.integer(unlist(cumsum(countrycounts$n)))+1
numbers[length(numbers)]<-numbers[length(numbers)]-1

#I pass this to my stan model in Rstan, and also pass J as an interger indicating the number of countries
#relevant stan model code:

model{
int row_mark; row_mark<-1;

  for (l in 1:numbers[1]){
y[l] ~ normal(intercept[1] + X[l]*beta,sigma);
  }

  for (j in 2:J){
    for(i in numbers[j-1]:numbers[j]){
y[i] ~ normal(intercept[j] + X[i]*beta,sigma);
  }
}
}

// Its easier to break it into two groups because of the way dplyr and the cumsum function work

