From ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com  Mon Jul 27 13:01:26 2015
From: ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com (Michael Betancourt)
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2015 13:01:26 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] problems with lognormal
Message-ID: <yahDp58t26Q.xfjFYAtPI5sJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <yahDp58t26Q.iTrck3s8JysJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <yahDp58t26Q.iTrck3s8JysJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/yahDp58t26Q/m/xfjFYAtPI5sJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

1) Tau is definitely not limited by the empirical bounds, which are random variables.
2) Use the lognormal directly.
3) Priors may help.  In particular, avoid hard constraints unless they’re physical
(i.e. lower=0 in sigma is good, but the upper=10 is not).

Try

data {
  int<lower=0> N0;
  vector<lower=0>[N0] y0;
  int<lower=0> N1;
  vector[N1] y1;
}

parameters {
  real<lower=0> sigma;
  real mu;
  real  tau;
}

model {
sigma ~ cauchy(0, 2.5);
tau ~ normal(0, 10);

y0 ~ lognormal(mu, sigma);
y1 ~ lognormal(mu + tau, sigma);
}

If you know that tau is always positive then you can define it with a lower=0
bound and switch the normal prior to a half-Cauchy prior.

