Sebastian, if I understand your question correctly, you shouldn't have to worry about thinning based on ESS; Instead, just calculate your quantity of interest in the generated quantities block (or after the fact in R) and use it however you would any other set of parameter draws--you can then directly assess the ESS of this generated quantity, check Rhat, etc...

Seth

On Fri, Jul 31, 2015 at 11:04 AM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
> Check out the problematic posteriors chapter of the manual
> for examples of just this.  What happens is that when two
> parameters are not well identified, some function of them may
> be much better identified and produce higher effective sample
> sizes.
>
> - Bob
>
> > On Jul 31, 2015, at 9:50 AM, Sebastian Weber <sdw....@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > Ok, fine. But what does that mean for derived quantities?
> >
